<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tullus Hostilius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 673–642 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tullus Hostilius was the third of the legendary kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, traditionally reigning from 673 to 642 BCE. Roman tradition portrayed him as a warlike king who fought Alba Longa, Fidenae and the Sabines; after the duel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatii-and-curiatii">Horatii and Curiatii</a> he destroyed Alba and moved its people to Rome. He was also credited with building the Curia Hostilia, the first senate house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius.jpg" alt="Tullus Hostilius" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille(1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulius-Hostilius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tullus Hostilius came from a Roman family of Alban descent. According to Dionysius of Halicarnassus, its earliest known member, Hostus Hostilius, had migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from Medullia and fell in the front ranks fighting beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> against the Sabines. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>, the Senate appointed interreges, each serving for five days, and the Curiate Assembly elected Tullus king by acclamation. He still needed the auspices, the sanction of the gods, before entering office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to Dionysius, Tullus began by dividing the royal estates among landless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. He brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelian-hill">Caelian Hill</a> within the walls and lived there for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records that after the conquest of Alba Longa he added ten squadrons of Albans to the cavalry, raising it from 300 to 600. Ancient sources credit him with the Curia Hostilia, the first senate house, and the Comitium, with the Fetial college, which conducted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s treaties and declarations of war, and with the Salii Collini, a second college of priests.</p><p>War with Alba Longa began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raids across the border. Rather than fight a full battle, the Alban dictator Mettius Fufetius proposed that three brothers from each city fight for supremacy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s champion, Publius Horatius, won. Later, in a war against Veii and Fidenae, Mettius held the Alban troops back on a hilltop and betrayed both sides. Tullus had him executed, destroyed Alba Longa, and brought its people to Rome as citizens. Leading Alban families, including the Julii, entered the Senate. Sources also record a Sabine war won at the Battle of the Malitiosa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says Tullus neglected the gods until omens and a pestilence came late in his reign. He fell ill, tried to perform rites from Numa's writings, and did the ceremony for Jupiter Elicius wrongly; lightning struck his house and burned it with his family. Dionysius gives another version, in which Ancus Marcius and his followers killed Tullus and his household on a stormy night.</p><p>Modern historians treat the early kings with scepticism, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Dionysius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> wrote at least five hundred years later. Most nonetheless accept that Tullus existed, because Tullus is a rare praenomen and the name Hostilius did not belong to a great Republican family. Some see his story as modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. Corneille's tragedy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> (1640) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-louis-david">Jacques-Louis David</a>'s Oath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatii-and-curiatii">Horatii</a> (1784) draw on the legend of the duel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tullus Hostilius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Livy, Rome under <b>Tullus Hostilius</b> defeated Alba after the duel of the Horatii and Curiatii in the mid-7th century BCE, razed the city and moved its people to the Caelian Hill.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li></ul></section>
